ound pages describe beans, nibs, chocolate liquor, cocoa butter, conching, white chocolate and the disputed ruby category. Three community pages contain only one planned local blind caramel comparison, place-specific hot-cocoa preferences and a lay discussion of smell, texture, color and temperature in flavor. No source supplies current delivered labels, complete ingredients and allergens for all offers, a matched bar tasting or a universal percentage-to-quality ranking.","target":"Audit what every frozen seller, concept and community source can and cannot establish for an affordable chocolate tasting. Build an exact-offer matrix with literal pack, mass, price, percentage, category, rating and unknown fields, show conditional arithmetic, and preserve all quantity conflicts. Explain the general bean-to-liquor pathway, cocoa solids and butter, and conching without inventing exact process histories or a longer-is-better rule. Explain why white chocolate contains a cocoa-derived fat despite lacking nonfat cocoa solids, why real-versus-fake depends on definitions and preferences, and why ruby remains a disputed category rather than a title-proven fourth natural type. Then design a small category-aware tasting that separates educational contrasts from matched comparisons, verifies guest and label constraints, masks cues, standardizes comparable servings and records sensory difference separately from liking. End with a lowest-commitment passing lineup, verified control, reduced lineup or deferral procedure without a universal product, percentage or category winner."},"graph_motif":"multi_branch_synthesis","graph_node_to_step_id":{"bridge_bean_to_liquor_process":"B1","bridge_category_aware_tasting_protocol":"B8","bridge_community_tasting_variable_scope":"B2","bridge_conching_texture_flavor":"B3","bridge_pack_mass_cost_normalization":"B4","bridge_percentage_component_quality":"B5","bridge_sampler_decision_preparation":"B9","bridge_seller_sampler_offer_scope":"B6","bridge_whi